_epa_per_play":0.239},{"week":14,"opponent":"CLE","home":true,"pf":23.0,"pa":10.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":0.03658,"pass_rate":0.6034,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6364,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1394},{"week":15,"opponent":"TB","home":false,"pf":34.0,"pa":23.0,"off_snaps":61,"epa_per_play":-0.01747,"pass_rate":0.6885,"pass_rate_neutral":0.7391,"def_epa_per_play":-0.1056},{"week":16,"opponent":"NE","home":false,"pf":22.0,"pa":18.0,"off_snaps":78,"epa_per_play":0.08047,"pass_rate":0.6923,"pass_rate_neutral":0.6875,"def_epa_per_play":-0.08766},{"week":18,"opponent":"BAL","home":true,"pf":27.0,"pa":16.0,"off_snaps":64,"epa_per_play":-0.1992,"pass_rate":0.6875,"pass_rate_neutral":0.75,"def_epa_per_play":-0.3193}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2023,"team":"WAS","distance":0.712,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"SEA","distance":0.7554,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"BUF","distance":0.8596,"shared":24,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.9365,"shared":21,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"IND","distance":0.9446,"shared":24,"play_caller":"Frank Reich"}],"defense":[{"season":2018,"team":"PHI","distance":0.5758,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"IND","distance":0.5888,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2025,"team":"JAX","distance":0.6037,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Anthony Campanile"},{"season":2019,"team":"NO","distance":0.6625,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2023,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6695,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Bengals went 12-4 in 2022 and outscored opponents 418 to 322. Both sides of the ball were strong, with the offense 5th in EPA per play and the defense 10th in EPA allowed. Their most distinctive traits were the fewest backs in the backfield and the 3rd fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Bengals offense was one of the best in the league by EPA per play, and it was much better passing the ball than running it. They threw more than almost anyone relative to the situation, even when the score did not call for it. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the fewest backs in the backfield and the 2nd lowest pre-snap motion rate in the league. Joe Burrow ran the passing game, with Ja'Marr Chase leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Bengals defense was well above average by EPA allowed per play. Its strongest area was pressure rate, where it ranked 5th, and its weakest was sack rate, where it ranked 29th. Trey Hendrickson was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Joseph Ossai, who had 3.5 sacks while playing only 32% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. In terms of scheme, they had the 3rd fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Bengals were one of the most conservative teams on fourth down, going for it on only 11% of their short-yardage chances. Their kickers made 83% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 27% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 90% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Joe Burrow played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 4,475 yards. Ja'Marr Chase was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 1,046 receiving yards. Joe Mixon handled most of the work in the backfield with 65% of the carries. On defense, Trey Hendrickson led the team with 8 sacks and Vonn Bell picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Zac Taylor called the offense as head coach and Lou Anarumo called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Commanders and the 2024 Seahawks. Their defense looked most like the 2018 Eagles and the 2025 Colts under Lou Anarumo.","trends":"The offense went from 12th in EPA per play in 2021 to 5th in 2022, a modest improvement. The defense moved from 13th in EPA allowed per play in 2021 to 10th in 2022, a modest improvement. The biggest change in approach was singleback usage, which went from 33% to 19%.","notes":"2022 is the first season with every data source available, including FTN's play design charting. It is also the last season that used the older formation labels, so singleback and I formation rates still appear here."}}